WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.280
- [Announcer] Middle Ground
is a social experiment

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.260
that brings humans with opposing beliefs

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.850
or experiences together.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.800
These discussion may contain viewpoints

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.660
that are the result of misinformation.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.000
Remember to seek out
experts and to be critical

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.310
of your own biases while
forming an opinion.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.770
Please see the humanity
in each participant,

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.143
and as always, we encourage empathy.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.410
- Two things can be true.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.450
You can have had a very hard time.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.070
Privilege is not just
limited to white people.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.990
But to say white privilege doesn't exist,

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.210
from historical context, to me,

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.050
is respectfully absurd.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:38.883
(pensive, enlightening music)

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.470
- [Moderator] Step forward
if you agree with the prompt.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.953
White people also experience racism.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.213
- I think racism is
definitely not a one race

00:01:05.213 --> 00:01:06.450
against the other.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:07.722
It goes all ways.

00:01:07.722 --> 00:01:09.600
It doesn't matter if you're
white, if you're black,

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.340
if you're Asian, it doesn't matter.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.170
You could experience racism

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.240
no matter what color your skin is.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.820
- I think that people have a propensity

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.460
to kind of have an aversion

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.020
towards what's different from them.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.420
I've been called a cracker before.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.300
I've been told I was
going to get beaten up

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.680
because I was white.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.710
I would argue that on a systemic level,

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.690
white people don't experience racism

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.080
the same way a lot of other
races experience racism.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.120
But I would say on an individual level,

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.240
any group is capable of being racist

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.160
towards another group as well.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.220
- So the argument against
this position frequently is

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.710
that racism is a system, not a sentiment

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:50.820
in the United States.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.530
And I agree with that really strongly.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.240
But at the same time, I'm Jewish.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.261
I'm racially white.

00:01:55.261 --> 00:01:58.740
And I've definitely experience
racism, discrimination.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.900
The paradox is that most of that racism

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.730
is coming from white
people or European society,

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:09.033
white nationalism, et cetera,
that's directed at Jews.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.090
I'm Dane, and I'm Jewish.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.920
I'm proud of being Jewish

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.970
and being connected an an
ancient religion and culture.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.210
But I'm not proud to be white I would say.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:25.530
White privilege, I think, is
an aspect of American society.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:26.610
I think it exists.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:27.930
When I go to a job interview,

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.330
I don't have to worry about my hairstyle

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:34.050
or the way I normally speak or my name.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.520
And that's something
perhaps a Black person

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.110
would have to worry about.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.233
- [Moderator] Can the
disagreers step forward?

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:44.970
- So I just wanna say

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.780
that I want to validate
everyone's individual experiences.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.580
I do also believe

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.360
that white people
experience bias, prejudice.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.554
But the reason I did not step forward

00:02:57.554 --> 00:03:00.360
is because by definition,

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:05.280
racism is tied to power and economics.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.150
You talked about systemic discrimination.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.640
I don't feel white
people experience racism

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.490
by definition and by actual concept.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.290
- What is the definition of racism

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:17.640
and who wrote that definition?

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:21.120
- In the late 1500s, early 1600s,

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.460
there was slavery, they
started bringing over slaves.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:27.600
But there was really no
concept of classifying people

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:28.433
in terms of race.

00:03:28.433 --> 00:03:29.760
It's just we're bringing these people over

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:30.960
and they're slaves.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.720
What ended up happening is
some of the slave owners,

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.580
who were white, started mixing
with some of the slaves.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.205
And now here you have these Black people

00:03:38.205 --> 00:03:41.640
that look like the people
that are owning things.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.050
So they're like, whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:48.050
The theory of race was formed
to classify a group of people

00:03:48.503 --> 00:03:52.110
who would be lesser than and
have less economic power.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:53.940
And this would be imposed against them

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.380
for hundreds of years.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:58.650
So that has not been the white
experience in this country.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.840
But it does not invalidate the things

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:01.830
that white people experience.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.780
It's just not racism by definition.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.960
Is it prejudice, is it
bias, is it wrong, yes.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:07.830
- Mine is simple.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.320
If one person treats
another person differently

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.820
because of the color of their skin,

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.080
to me, that's racism.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.820
I will tell you that I
was stopped by the police

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.866
plenty of times when I was a young man.

00:04:16.866 --> 00:04:19.650
And I will also tell
you that I then decided

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:22.050
to spend 35 years as a policeman.

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.536
I'll tell you that I stopped lots of kids

00:04:24.536 --> 00:04:26.400
coming home from school.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.800
Depending who it was that I stopped,

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.547
the first thing out of
their mouth would be,

00:04:30.547 --> 00:04:32.730
"You only stopped me because I'm Black."

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:33.723
And I would say,

00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:37.380
"You're driving a sports car
with a window that's this big.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:38.700
I can't see who's driving.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.250
And I stopped you because
you're doing 95 in a 55.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.790
I had no idea who I was pulling
over until I stopped you."

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:49.020
Now, if I treat you differently
because you're Black,

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:50.430
then I'm a racist.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:51.666
But if I write you the same ticket

00:04:51.666 --> 00:04:53.070
that I was gonna write you

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:55.623
before I knew who was
behind the wheel, I'm not.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:04:59.250
- So I'm glad you used that example,

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.410
and I want to commend you for
your service to our country.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:02.820
But I do want-
- But.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:03.990
- But I do wanna say,

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.720
'cause I've actually
taught classes for the LAPD

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.420
and for their cadets about these issues.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.120
But what I can say, which is a statistic,

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.740
and which is also a fact,

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.934
is that 60% of our prison population

00:05:15.934 --> 00:05:18.872
is African American-
- Different issue.

00:05:18.872 --> 00:05:20.700
- Yes, but let me finish, please.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:23.160
Is African American males, right?

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.260
When you go into a system

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:28.230
and you are kept in that system

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:31.290
and you're cycled through probation

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:34.470
and there is a systematic
sequence of oppression,

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:36.240
that's not happening to white males.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.730
So it's bigger than just the stop itself.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.500
It's the system.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:41.670
My name is Sierra.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:45.210
I'm an actor, a speaker,
and a human rights activist.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.420
I'm very proud to be African
American, to be Black.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:53.420
The challenges that come with being Black,

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:55.260
also being a Black woman, are very real.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:56.580
I'm at a place in my life

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:00.420
where I'm acknowledging
these things are real.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:02.400
Discrimination is real.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.730
The systematic oppression of
Black people is very real.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.610
But I will succeed in spite of that.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:12.363
- [Moderator] The police force
is foundationally racist.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:23.490
- I want to start off by saying

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:26.580
I don't think that every cop
out there today is racist.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:27.978
- Same.
- Yes.

00:06:27.978 --> 00:06:28.811
- I think there are a lot of them

00:06:28.811 --> 00:06:30.240
who are really doing good work.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.990
But I know from the
establishment of the policing

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:35.010
in the United States

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:37.710
that is directly from the slave catchers

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:40.320
who were hired to catch runaway slaves

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:41.400
and bring them back home.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.590
- Yeah, I agree that
all cops aren't racist.

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:47.160
I had plenty of interactions
with police that were fine.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.850
But I was told by a former police officer

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:53.790
who was a cop who decided he
didn't wanna do that anymore

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:57.300
that the training is
pretty much specialized

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.790
for only colored people,
especially Black people,

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:02.370
that if you're around them,
they're the most dangerous

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:04.770
so you have to act in certain situations,

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:06.690
or react to certain situations.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:08.430
- I think that I just
really want to emphasize

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:11.340
that I really think that
there are some officers

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:14.610
who are white that don't even believe

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:18.660
that they're doing anything
that may be racially motivated

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:21.810
because they're simply doing
what they were taught to do.

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:23.580
- I come from a police family.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:25.119
And I'd like to add they do offer

00:07:25.119 --> 00:07:28.200
diversity trainings in the academy.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.360
So either it needs to be more extensive.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.460
Or maybe we need to take it a step back

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:35.580
and fix the selection process,

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.970
because that process could
be inherently racist.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:41.280
- Hello, my name is Nia, and I am Black.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.890
I'm proud of my race because
when I look back historically

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:47.340
at what people of my
heritage have gone through

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.530
and to see where we are
now, it's remarkable.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:52.290
And it definitely pushes me
to continue to be resilient

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:53.690
and innovative as they were.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:04.230
- Historically, people
formed groups, villages.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:07.290
Some people were hunters,
some people were farmers.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:11.430
And then you had whoever
was gonna protect the group.

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:14.490
And over hundreds of
years, thousands of years,

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:18.540
whatever it is, that's
how we wind up having

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:22.470
cops, firemen, armies, and navies.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:26.550
Community policing as a
theory is a good idea.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:28.770
As a practice, it has some hiccups.

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:31.320
When I show up at your house

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:34.620
because you and your husband got into it

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.774
and he beats you up

00:08:36.774 --> 00:08:39.750
and I'm friends with him.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.330
So do I want to take him to jail?

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:44.040
He's my friend.

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:47.460
Or I live in the same neighborhood

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:52.460
and I go deal with some
guy who's a really bad dude

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:56.550
and he says, "I know where you live.

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:58.350
I know where your kids are."

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:03.350
So to some degree, we have
to have a little separation.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.300
This is gonna get into a hot button topic.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:10.800
But I will tell you that when
I watched the horrible video

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:14.220
of George Floyd, I will
tell you that I can justify

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.710
many of the things that happened,

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:21.710
but I cannot justify nine
minutes on his neck or back.

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:25.893
But what I can also tell you
is that I as a policeman,

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:29.550
and this is my personal opinion only, too,

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:32.820
the officer who was on
him for nine minutes,

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:37.770
he probably spent too much
time in the same neighborhood

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:42.770
dealing with the same
problems over and over again,

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:44.820
that he became jaded.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:47.134
- Isn't that was being
a police officer is?

00:09:47.134 --> 00:09:49.950
- It's a social service job.
- That's the job.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:52.740
- And you're assigned to that
district or that community.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:54.420
- But I think you need to move around

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:56.820
so that you get to see other things,

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:58.470
you don't become that jaded.

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:01.260
- The police, no matter what your race is,

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:03.390
we have to be integrated all
together with the community.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:04.950
It doesn't have to be community policing.

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:06.600
But we all have to
understand each other more.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:09.480
I think cultural competency
would be very helpful.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:10.313
Like, that's my issue.

00:10:10.313 --> 00:10:13.184
Whether or not the foundations
of the police force

00:10:13.184 --> 00:10:16.350
was or still is, was racist,

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:17.820
now we can change that.

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:19.080
But it starts with cops.

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:20.040
Like, at the end of the day,

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:23.490
it's the bad police
officers that are issue.

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:24.990
Not all police officers,

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:26.610
but the bad police officers,

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:27.480
we need to vet them out.

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:28.530
And the good police officers,

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:31.320
we need to encourage them
to be amongst the community.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:32.760
- How do we vet them out?

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:34.830
- By making the police unions accountable.

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:37.860
Because I feel like that
demographic of officers,

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:39.450
they're actually making things a lot worse

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:40.740
for the other officers

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:42.679
that are actually just trying-
- Absolutely.

00:10:42.679 --> 00:10:44.370
- to do their job.

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:47.250
I think we need to get some
type of real accountability

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:49.710
that I don't think the police
should be policing themselves.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:52.440
So when those cops that aren't doing

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:53.700
according to their oath,

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:55.290
they can be properly removed

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:58.380
instead of getting all of these passes.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:01.050
- The bad police
officers, I hate the term.

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:02.850
Like the bad apple.

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:05.160
It's like, no, you're a faulty grenade.

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:09.240
Like you are ruining your
whole department reputation.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:11.850
I feel like I was held
up to a higher standards

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:14.190
working at Whole Foods sometimes.

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:17.190
I got three chances, (snap) out.

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:20.593
But Derek had so many-
- 17 reports before

00:11:20.593 --> 00:11:22.230
George Floyd.
- 17 reports!

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:24.000
- It's like get him out of there!

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:25.530
What are you guys doing?

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:26.667
- Do you think police officers,

00:11:26.667 --> 00:11:27.960
and I genuinely am curious.

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:30.600
Do you think they should get paid more?

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:32.520
- I think police officers
should get paid more.

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:35.160
I also think police
officers should do less.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:39.540
Why do I get sent to all
of the mental health cases?

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:41.130
Why do I get sent
- I agree.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:42.690
to all off these things.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:45.270
Now, I'll tell you why I get sent to them

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:48.240
is because we'll stop
sending the police there

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.730
and then something horrible
and tragic is gonna happen

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:52.830
and they're gonna say, "Why
didn't we have a cop there?"

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:54.960
- Do you think we need to
have different divisions,

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:57.420
like mental health, and just other,

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:00.600
or maybe even specific police
that are just for the schools?

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:03.600
Do you think that would help
with a lot of the issues?

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:06.825
- No, we do have
specialized police officers

00:12:06.825 --> 00:12:08.700
in certain things.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:11.220
But if we keep specializing

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:13.710
and we get so compartmentalized

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:17.190
that I can't go, I'm right
across the street from you

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:18.630
when you're screaming for help.

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:20.326
- Oh, I see what you're saying.

00:12:20.326 --> 00:12:21.540
- Yeah, that's not what I do.

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:23.910
Gotta find the guy that
deals with screaming women,

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:25.620
or whatever it is.

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:26.790
My name is Jimmy,

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:29.880
and I am a retired police officer.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:34.054
I'm not gonna tell you
that there are no racists

00:12:34.054 --> 00:12:36.210
on the police department.

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:39.850
I don't think Blacks were
historically treated fairly

00:12:39.850 --> 00:12:41.430
under the law.

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:45.840
I think that we have made
great progress, great strides.

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:49.110
I think we have to continue to get better.

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:53.820
But I also think we probably
see racism to this day

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:56.013
in places where it maybe isn't.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:00.843
- [Moderator] I sometimes
wish I was a different race.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:10.770
- I'd say not so much these days,

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:13.890
but definitely 100% when I was a kid,

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:15.210
I wished I was white.

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:16.827
I grew up with my white mom

00:13:16.827 --> 00:13:19.200
and I didn't see my
Black side of my family

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:21.000
after a certain point.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:22.170
I grew up in a small town.

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:24.240
I was often the only student of color,

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:26.160
definitely the only Black kid.

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:27.390
And I had this kinky hair.

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:29.250
I couldn't figure out how to work with it.

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:31.980
And everybody also would call me an Oreo.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:34.230
It was always just being othered.

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:37.620
And so being a kid, you know,
you just want to fit in.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.330
And for me, I wanted to be white.

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:41.010
I just wanted to look like my mom,

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:42.597
I want the nice straight hair,

00:13:42.597 --> 00:13:45.720
and I just wanted to feel, like, included.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:49.083
- Yeah, like I remember
in elementary school,

00:13:49.083 --> 00:13:52.290
like I was thinking, no
man is ever gonna like me

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:54.240
because I freaking look like this.

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:57.000
And I just remember hating my hair

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:59.280
'cause as soon as puberty hit,

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:00.870
it was just (blows out forcefully).

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:02.370
It just blew up.

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:03.427
And people kept on asking,

00:14:03.427 --> 00:14:05.880
"Do you even need a
pillow to sleep at night?"

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:08.370
I'm like, I can't stand these questions.

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:09.810
I can't do this.

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:12.930
I'm Jordan, and I am biracial.

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:14.485
I think being biracial is definitely

00:14:14.485 --> 00:14:16.890
like living in the in-between

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:19.680
because culturally, yes I grew up white

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:21.270
and I completely know that.

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:23.430
Like I was raised by my white mother.

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:27.630
But then there's also a part
where race is perceived.

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:30.600
And it's what people put on you

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:34.203
and how they respond to
you in your everyday life.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:43.710
- It's so interesting because I grew up

00:14:43.710 --> 00:14:46.080
in predominantly non-Black communities.

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:48.940
And for some reason, still,
like being the minority,

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:53.040
at home in the family as
well in school and the world,

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:55.320
I was always so proud to be Black.

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:58.020
I actually really yearned to
be around other Black people.

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:00.300
So thankfully in college,
that was an experience

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:02.515
where even though we made
up a small community,

00:15:02.515 --> 00:15:06.055
I was still able to, okay, this
is like people look like me.

00:15:06.055 --> 00:15:09.060
- I was in the military for six years.

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:12.660
And I was usually the
only Jew in my battalion.

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:15.180
And I think that isolation produced

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:17.740
a sort of pride and
understanding of who I am

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:19.673
and where I come from.

00:15:19.673 --> 00:15:23.700
- I have never wanted
to be anything different

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:24.990
than what I was.

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:26.910
But that's because I really
never thought about it.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:30.660
And to your old point,
maybe, I don't know,

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:32.670
but maybe people treated you that way

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:36.900
because there is this
sense of wanting something

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:39.000
that I haven't ever had before.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:40.373
I'd love to have a Lamborghini.

00:15:40.373 --> 00:15:41.670
You know what I mean?

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:44.490
It's different than any
other car I've ever had.

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:46.710
It's different than what I grew up with.

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:48.090
- I think personally for me,

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:51.730
I mean, I was actually
adopted by white parents

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:54.000
in a very diverse family.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:57.750
I think six out of the
eight of us are all adopted,

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:58.590
all different races.

00:15:58.590 --> 00:16:02.370
I kind of have my own story
apart from everyone else.

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:04.020
And the same for everyone else
that's adopted in my family,

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:05.280
they have their own story.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:07.290
I always have pride in
it, but at the same time,

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:08.160
my parents are my parents,

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:09.120
no matter the color of their skin.

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:10.499
It's the people who raised me.

00:16:10.499 --> 00:16:12.120
They raised me to just not look at it

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:13.560
through the lens of racism.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:14.670
That's what I've always done.

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:17.970
And I never wanted to
change my race at all.

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:20.220
- I'm Joshua and I'm Black.

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:23.190
I've never experienced racism.

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:25.860
I've experienced stuff from
people from my own race.

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:27.570
I grew up in a white
family by white parents.

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:29.850
I was constantly called white-washed.

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:33.570
It upset me, but I
didn't let it get to me.

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:36.930
Being Black in America to me is just being

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:39.813
an American in America,
just like any other person.

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:43.083
- [Moderator] White privilege exists.

00:16:51.011 --> 00:16:51.844
- I mean, America was
founded by white people

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:55.890
for white people at the time.

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:57.660
All of the institutions were designed

00:16:57.660 --> 00:16:59.520
to benefit white people.

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:03.030
So I mean, of course
white privilege lingers

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:04.590
into society today.

00:17:04.590 --> 00:17:06.030
We can't deny that.

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:09.540
- Where I would argue with
you just a little bit is

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:13.020
I think we throw out
the term white privilege

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:14.460
far too often for things

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:16.530
that aren't necessarily white privilege.

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:19.260
- I got to agree with
you complete on that one.

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:22.380
I think there's generational
white privilege for sure.

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:26.160
But I think now it's
almost used as a reason

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:29.610
to not try as hard.

00:17:29.610 --> 00:17:32.610
If we stop doing that, just
like really work for it

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:35.130
and not use white privilege as an excuse,

00:17:35.130 --> 00:17:38.550
then we could really get
somewhere in the Black community.

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:41.430
- I would respectfully disagree.

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:44.640
Like, all of that is true to an extent.

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:47.400
Like, I graduated university
during the pandemic,

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:48.420
the height of it.

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:50.190
I went abroad for university.

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:51.030
I got two degrees.

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:52.980
I graduated with honors.

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:54.540
I learned to speak two languages.

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:56.700
Should have been able to
get a job right away, right?

00:17:56.700 --> 00:18:00.420
No, it took me nine months
before I was able to get a job.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:02.501
Had to go completely outside of my field.

00:18:02.501 --> 00:18:05.570
But then when I looked at
all of my white colleagues

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:07.028
who are in my program,

00:18:07.028 --> 00:18:09.030
all of them were hired immediately.

00:18:09.030 --> 00:18:11.700
- I need to introduce you to my daughter.

00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:14.948
She's white, took her a long time

00:18:14.948 --> 00:18:15.990
out of college.
- And I'm not saying that.

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:17.580
- I'm saying that-
- She didn't get her job

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:19.180
in what her degree is in either.

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:22.410
- I respect that, but my point is saying,

00:18:22.410 --> 00:18:25.290
me comparing myself to the white students

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:27.352
who were directly in my position,

00:18:27.352 --> 00:18:29.490
they're successful, I'm not.

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:33.630
- This country was founded
upon white people coming

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:36.780
and then putting in act
Jim Crow and other laws,

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:39.570
saying that if you are not white,

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:41.460
you are genetically inferior.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:42.960
You are not even a person.

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:45.237
And some of those laws are still,

00:18:45.237 --> 00:18:47.790
and those systems are
still in place today.

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:49.230
Two things can be true.

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:51.240
You can have had a very hard time.

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:52.890
Privilege is not just
limited to white people.

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:54.780
But to say white privilege doesn't exist

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:59.040
from a historical context,
to me, respectfully absurd.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:01.710
- Something that I
learned about recently is

00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:04.260
the privilege that exists
in the medical field,

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:07.419
specifically relating
to the birthing process

00:19:07.419 --> 00:19:10.170
in giving epidurals.

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:13.950
There was a study that's
recently, or was debunked,

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:17.947
that said Black people have
a higher tolerance for pain,

00:19:17.947 --> 00:19:21.990
which then has been falsely used by people

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:24.840
who help support births in being hesitant

00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:29.430
to provide the epidurals for
Black women giving birth.

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:32.130
A lot of women die in childbirth.

00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:33.960
I don't know the exact statistics.

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:36.090
- Three times more likely,
Black women are to die.

00:19:36.090 --> 00:19:36.923
- Thank you.

00:19:36.923 --> 00:19:39.540
The fact that I will be taken seriously,

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:42.390
more seriously than a
Black woman in my place,

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:44.820
if I should be giving birth at some point,

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:48.630
I see that as just an absolute privilege.

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:50.760
My name is Bryn and I just graduated

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:52.170
and I'm currently getting hours

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:54.330
to becoming a licensed therapist.

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:57.780
As a therapist, in school,
we're taught to approach things

00:19:57.780 --> 00:19:59.850
with what's called cultural humility.

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:01.830
Cultural humility is being curious.

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:04.770
And instead of trying
to have all the answers,

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:06.891
working collaboratively with the client

00:20:06.891 --> 00:20:10.540
to kind of understand
where they're coming from.

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:13.983
- [Moderator] I have been
fetishized for my race.

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:22.627
- You know, the standard one.

00:20:22.627 --> 00:20:24.420
"You're pretty for a Black girl."

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:26.940
I'm like, that's not a compliment.

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:29.282
That's so weird to say.

00:20:29.282 --> 00:20:31.320
And one of the go-to pickup lines for men,

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:34.470
they're like, "Oh my God, what are you?"

00:20:34.470 --> 00:20:36.660
I'm like, this is not cute.

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:38.370
I'm tired of having this conversation.

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:39.300
This is lame.

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:42.000
Yes, I know I have green eyes, hazel eyes.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:44.340
Like, let's move on.

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:46.410
- I think Black women are, in particular,

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:49.440
just extremely sexualized and fetishized.

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:51.330
Every day I go out,

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:54.150
I'm making sure that my
outfit is, you know, okay,

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:56.730
because I don't wanna
draw certain attention.

00:20:56.730 --> 00:20:59.310
The way I wear my hair,
if it's in an Afro,

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:01.080
if it's in puffs, if it's in braids,

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.210
I'm gonna get treated differently.

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:06.390
Yeah, every single day, something happens.

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:09.690
- So there's a form of
positive anti-Semitism

00:21:09.690 --> 00:21:13.110
where Jews are perceived
as being put together,

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:16.350
coming from money, being doctors, lawyers.

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:19.650
And you know, women are attracted
to those sort of things.

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:22.560
And also, too, like all
Jewish men are circumcised.

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:24.168
Some women have that preference.

00:21:24.168 --> 00:21:26.490
So I've definitely experienced it.

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:29.580
But I feel like it's a sort
of subtle, trivial thing.

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:32.133
Like it never really bothered me?

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:36.090
But it's occurred nonetheless.

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:38.340
- Yeah, I've had experiences
in high school, college,

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:41.047
you know, people ask me
to stop me, mainly women,

00:21:41.047 --> 00:21:41.880
"Oh, are you Egyptian?"

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:44.040
"Are you Black and this?"
or "Black and that?"

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:46.290
It's always Black and something else.

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:49.087
And I let them know what
I am, and they be like,

00:21:49.087 --> 00:21:52.623
"Ooh, that's exotic," or
something crazy, you know?

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:55.860
My name is Dino, and I'm biracial.

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:57.420
I don't want people to think

00:21:57.420 --> 00:22:00.930
that I'm like split between
being Black or white.

00:22:00.930 --> 00:22:03.780
I'm acknowledge that I'm
mixed and I'm biracial.

00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:05.610
But for the most part growing up,

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:06.930
I was always considered Black.

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:07.920
I considered myself Black.

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:08.910
My family did as well.

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:11.730
So I don't think we should
live in a color-blind society.

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:13.710
But I also don't think
we should stay stuck

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:16.560
and feel like we can't move
forward and still thrive

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:19.170
and accomplish things
because of our skin color

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:20.620
and where we live in America.

00:22:25.260 --> 00:22:26.490
- I would just have a question

00:22:26.490 --> 00:22:29.130
for everyone who stepped forward.

00:22:29.130 --> 00:22:32.580
I'm just curious, how
that's like, what's it like?

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:35.430
- Exhausting.
- It sounds exhausting.

00:22:35.430 --> 00:22:37.140
- I think Black women historically

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:40.350
have a really interesting
relationship with desirability.

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:42.420
Culturally it seems we're not desired.

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:44.580
And then when we are,
we're being fetishized,

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:46.410
so it's like, okay, we're
usually not desired,

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:47.243
but now we are.

00:22:47.243 --> 00:22:49.680
Am I supposed to lean
into this fetishization?

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:51.660
- I think just being a woman,

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:54.300
we deal with certain things every day.

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:57.720
Specifically being a Black
woman and being fetishized,

00:22:57.720 --> 00:22:59.190
it's interesting because if I,

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:00.210
especially in my industry,

00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:02.040
'cause I'm there to work
and be professional,

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:04.170
I'm just super professional

00:23:04.170 --> 00:23:06.420
and I don't give any
attention that at all,

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:07.473
that kind of energy,

00:23:07.473 --> 00:23:10.050
then I'm an angry Black woman.

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:12.933
So I think that it's just really-

00:23:13.890 --> 00:23:14.910
- You can't win.

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:16.920
- It does feel like that, so, yeah,

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:18.540
that you can't win sometimes.

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:19.770
- And there's also a flip side to it.

00:23:19.770 --> 00:23:21.660
It's like, for me, specifically,

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:24.884
I'm light-skinned amongst
majority dark-skinned people.

00:23:24.884 --> 00:23:27.990
And people deem me or
deem light-skinned people

00:23:27.990 --> 00:23:30.510
as a certain way, as
either weaker or less than.

00:23:30.510 --> 00:23:32.790
Or just like, okay, you're lighter than,

00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:34.230
so when we're so pro-Black

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:37.410
that we're never gonna
consider you as one of us.

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:38.243
You're just other.

00:23:38.243 --> 00:23:40.470
- A lot like touches
just on beauty standards

00:23:40.470 --> 00:23:43.020
in the Black community,
and that's colorism.

00:23:43.020 --> 00:23:44.497
Like I've even been told,

00:23:44.497 --> 00:23:46.770
"You're beautiful, you're not Black."

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:48.377
It's just like these little things.

00:23:48.377 --> 00:23:49.210
- Oh yeah.
- Or, you must be mixed

00:23:49.210 --> 00:23:50.613
with something, right?
- Yes.

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:53.850
- My name's Christina, and I'm biracial.

00:23:53.850 --> 00:23:57.210
I have been fetishized
for the color of my skin

00:23:57.210 --> 00:23:58.309
on both sides.

00:23:58.309 --> 00:24:02.070
Black men will be really into
me because I'm light-skinned.

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:05.310
Or other men who are not
Black might be into me

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:07.590
because I am that Black girl,

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:10.080
but maybe I'm not too Black.

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:12.690
And that, for whatever
reason, is appealing.

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:15.870
Being deemed as exotic or something other

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:17.430
is just very problematic.

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:19.950
And that plays into a
lot of issues of colorism

00:24:19.950 --> 00:24:22.080
within the Black community at large.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:25.293
- [Moderator] We need reparations
for the Black community.

00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:35.100
- I don't think people need
like individualized paychecks.

00:24:35.100 --> 00:24:37.410
Like I don't think that's gonna like work.

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:41.100
But I think pouring funds into communities

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:45.792
who could really use
it, building jobs there,

00:24:45.792 --> 00:24:47.970
more education there,

00:24:47.970 --> 00:24:51.299
like more opportunities would do so much.

00:24:51.299 --> 00:24:54.540
That's how I see it should be.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:56.460
- Any time that Black
communities in the US

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:59.640
tried to pull themselves
up by the bootstraps,

00:24:59.640 --> 00:25:00.870
they were attacked.

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:04.980
Like I think it was in
Tulsa, they bombed the city?

00:25:04.980 --> 00:25:07.290
Black Wall Street, it
was completely destroyed.

00:25:07.290 --> 00:25:09.900
So again, something needs to be done.

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:10.800
- When the Civil War ended,

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:12.060
there was a representative from Ohio.

00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:13.590
I can't remember his name.

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:15.040
But he was a radical Republican

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:18.577
and advocated for the phrase,

00:25:18.577 --> 00:25:21.930
"40 acres and a mule for
every emancipated slave."

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:24.180
So there was supposed to be reparations.

00:25:24.180 --> 00:25:25.410
And there never was.

00:25:25.410 --> 00:25:26.940
And I think especially as a Jewish person,

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:29.490
I mean, Jews received
reparations for the Holocaust

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:31.097
and are still receiving that.

00:25:31.097 --> 00:25:34.170
And in my mind, there's no amount of money

00:25:34.170 --> 00:25:38.010
that will ever undo the
wrong by the Holocaust.

00:25:38.010 --> 00:25:40.320
All the money in the world
will never make it right.

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:45.320
But I think the idea and
principle of reparations is good.

00:25:45.930 --> 00:25:48.203
Logistically, I don't
know how it would happen.

00:25:53.670 --> 00:25:55.470
- I just wanted to say
the reason I stayed back

00:25:55.470 --> 00:25:57.240
was because I wasn't entirely sure

00:25:57.240 --> 00:25:59.940
what was meant by reparations.

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:01.830
But your example of the Holocaust,

00:26:01.830 --> 00:26:03.150
I would say that I agree.

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:05.640
- Some other reason, when
I think of reparations,

00:26:05.640 --> 00:26:08.070
I think of something
similar to a stimulus check.

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:11.070
My worry is with the lack
of financial literacy,

00:26:11.070 --> 00:26:12.900
will we actually make strides.

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:16.380
That's my, so I would love
the idea of investing money

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:18.990
into programs in underserved communities,

00:26:18.990 --> 00:26:19.823
something like that.

00:26:19.823 --> 00:26:22.260
But I do have a fear that
even if we did have the money,

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:25.050
without that generational knowledge

00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:27.180
of how to take care of our finances,

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:30.000
that it would just deplete
and defeat the purpose.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:32.460
- But even if that were to happen, though,

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:35.190
I still think that that would
still be the right step.

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:38.880
You know, we'll give these
people the funds, or whatever.

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:40.380
We'll give them our promise.

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:44.070
And if they float or sink
or whatever, that's on them.

00:26:44.070 --> 00:26:47.629
At least we as a country
took the right step.

00:26:47.629 --> 00:26:51.030
- I'm all for money
going to the community.

00:26:51.030 --> 00:26:51.900
I am for that,

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:54.510
because the communities right
now are the way they are

00:26:54.510 --> 00:26:56.370
because of things in the past.

00:26:56.370 --> 00:26:59.040
But to give out checks
to individual people,

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:01.230
there's a difference between
learning from the past

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:03.930
and then still getting
caught up in the past.

00:27:03.930 --> 00:27:05.280
I mean, if I had a time machine,

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:07.173
I could go back in time and be like,

00:27:07.173 --> 00:27:10.290
hey, you never gave us
the 40 acres and a mule.

00:27:10.290 --> 00:27:12.420
I would be all for that
and I would be on that

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:14.400
for the people who were affected

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:16.350
and actually went through slavery.

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:19.440
But in today's age, like
no one's experienced it

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:20.273
and gone through it.

00:27:20.273 --> 00:27:25.273
Yes, communities are taking
hit by that, but I never-

00:27:25.950 --> 00:27:28.170
- You don't think your
ancestors experienced slavery?

00:27:28.170 --> 00:27:30.150
- I think they do, but I haven't.

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:31.290
I've never been in it,

00:27:31.290 --> 00:27:33.420
so why should I get a
check from somebody else

00:27:33.420 --> 00:27:34.418
who never put.

00:27:34.418 --> 00:27:37.710
You should be going
way, way, like way back.

00:27:37.710 --> 00:27:40.980
- So what about the people
who are the descendants

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:43.350
of the slave owners who are still reaping

00:27:43.350 --> 00:27:46.320
the financial benefits of your ancestors

00:27:46.320 --> 00:27:47.970
who slaved in those fields,

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:50.460
for literally generation after generation?

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:52.890
You don't think that during that time,

00:27:52.890 --> 00:27:55.410
your great-grandfather could
have been building a business

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:57.330
that could be flourishing by now,

00:27:57.330 --> 00:27:59.310
and you would have a
multimillion-dollar franchise,

00:27:59.310 --> 00:28:00.900
as they do off the backs of others?

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:03.480
You don't think that deserves
some rectification for you

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:04.503
in the present time?

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:09.570
- To be honest, no, because again,

00:28:09.570 --> 00:28:10.800
it's something that happened in the past.

00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:12.810
Again, I haven't experienced it.

00:28:12.810 --> 00:28:14.640
I'm doing fine right now, I'm doing fine.

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:16.800
And I'm not going to be all upset

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:20.130
over somebody else's ancestors

00:28:20.130 --> 00:28:22.586
who I don't even know who
enslaved my ancestors.

00:28:22.586 --> 00:28:23.820
I don't need it.

00:28:23.820 --> 00:28:25.050
I have no reason to have it.

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:27.480
- So for me, personally,
I'm not upset about it,

00:28:27.480 --> 00:28:30.720
but I do feel my grandfather, on his side,

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:32.430
they were the first family
in the state of Texas

00:28:32.430 --> 00:28:34.410
who actually filed for reparations.

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:37.890
And for me, I'm succeeding
in spite of that.

00:28:37.890 --> 00:28:39.630
But to this gentleman's point,

00:28:39.630 --> 00:28:41.227
when the government says,

00:28:41.227 --> 00:28:43.740
"Hey, we're gonna do a thing,"

00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:47.250
obviously the government
in itself saw the weight

00:28:47.250 --> 00:28:49.980
and the error of wrong, just
as the German government did.

00:28:49.980 --> 00:28:52.590
I think if I wanna buy 90 gold
chains, that's my business.

00:28:52.590 --> 00:28:54.150
I think it would be unfortunate.

00:28:54.150 --> 00:28:56.790
But I think if I'm entitled to something,

00:28:56.790 --> 00:28:58.680
I think it should be in the same fashion

00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:00.510
that the people of Jewish descent got it.

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:02.250
I think it should be in the same fashion

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:05.490
that the people that are Japanese got it.

00:29:05.490 --> 00:29:08.070
So you might think it doesn't affect you.

00:29:08.070 --> 00:29:13.070
But overall, I think we
have been greatly impacted

00:29:13.186 --> 00:29:16.050
by slavery, even up to current.

00:29:16.050 --> 00:29:19.230
I just choose not to focus on it nonstop

00:29:19.230 --> 00:29:22.230
so it doesn't become
something that holds me back.

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:23.700
But I definitely think it's something

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:25.380
that we're entitled to.

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:26.559
- Thank you.

00:29:26.559 --> 00:29:29.160
- Great, guys.
(applauding)

00:29:29.160 --> 00:29:31.021
(panelists talking over each other)

00:29:31.021 --> 00:29:33.021
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

00:29:36.228 --> 00:29:40.061
(pensive, enlightening music)

